---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Download — Remote for OpenCode"
  description="Install the Remote for OpenCode Mac companion via Homebrew or a direct download, then pair your iPhone."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <h1>Download</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Two pieces: a companion on your Mac, and the app on your iPhone.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>1. The Mac companion</h2>
    <p>Homebrew is the easiest route — it keeps itself up to date afterwards.</p>
    <pre><code>brew install --cask tjameswilliams/tap/remote-for-opencode</code></pre>
    <p>Or download it directly:</p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button" href="/downloads/RemoteForOpenCode.dmg">Download .dmg</a>
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
      Requires macOS 14 Sonoma or later, and
      <a href="https://opencode.ai">OpenCode</a> — the companion will tell you
      if it can't find it. Signed and notarized by Apple.
    </p>

    <h2>2. The iPhone app</h2>
    <p>
      On the App Store. It does nothing on its own — it needs the companion
      above to talk to.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Pair them</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Open the companion on your Mac and choose <strong>Pair iPhone…</strong></li>
      <li>Open the app on your iPhone.</li>
      <li>Confirm both screens show the same six-digit code.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Both devices need to be signed into the same iCloud account — that is
      how they find each other without you configuring anything.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
